<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The adaptive icon, which is what Android 8 and later actually draws.

  Without this file the launcher falls back to the legacy `ic_launcher.png`
  bitmap: a fixed square that modern launchers cannot mask to their shape and
  cannot animate, so Parentix sat as a hard-edged tile among circles. The
  per-density `ic_launcher_foreground.png` files existed all along and nothing
  referenced them — this is the reference.

  Background is a flat colour rather than a bitmap because the foreground is the
  whole icon: `ic_launcher_foreground.png` is the drawn tile, sized to exactly
  the 72dp the launcher shows, so the only thing behind it is the sliver the
  parallax wobble uncovers. Teal is what should be found there.

  `monochrome` is a *different* file from the foreground, which is the one thing
  here that is easy to get wrong. It was the same file back when the foreground
  was a white shield on nothing — a silhouette is exactly what a themed icon
  wants. The foreground is now full-colour artwork, and a themed icon keeps only
  the alpha channel, so pointing this at it would tint a solid rounded square and
  put a blank tile on the home screen of every phone with themed icons on.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/iconBackground"/>
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground"/>
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome"/>
</adaptive-icon>
